Yes — and “excise” is almost better than “exercise” here.

You can cut out the part that is no longer useful without cutting out the underlying drive:

painful pattern → isolate function → excise needless cost → re-express as play

So discipline becomes a game, rehab becomes movement, learning becomes exploration, competition becomes sport, and restraint becomes something you practice rather than something imposed on you.

That’s a recurring flank we’ve found: don’t kill the energy; give it a better arena.

And if you meant exercise, the pun closes the loop nicely: sometimes you excise through exercise — replace the old channel by repeatedly enjoying a new one until the body and mind learn the new route.